Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Gauge Twelve Leaves the Kick Blank
- Story & Context: At 118 BPM, Maialen opens with a two-hit low-high txalaparta proposition. Iker answers one eighth-note earlier than Nele's programmed kick on beat three. Instead of shifting the response, Nele executes Courtesy Cut and removes the kick entirely. The absence becomes the track's hook. A second cycle uses a LIGHT answer on a shorter plank, allowing the kick to return while the high wood tail clears. Leire adds one prepared-Pianet suspended-second shell only after both players complete the phrase. Jon replaces every third digital click with a muted pandero rim stroke. Klara enters in the first long Grain Carry section with an air-heavy low fundamental while the synth bass drops out.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Rainfall gauge marked 12 beside a printed rhythm chart with one kick square deliberately left blank, two wet wooden planks crossing the page like measurement lines, graphite notes bleeding slightly into damp paper. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “gauge twelve,” “leave the kick,” “your space,” and “I answer.”

Track 2: Two Calls Owed in the West Room
- Story & Context: At 122 BPM, the track foregrounds Counter Ledger. Maialen delivers two short calls without full replies while Iker remains on soft upper-plank taps. The ledger reaches two unanswered marks, forcing Iker to open the next section alone with a HEAVY three-strike low-plank figure. Nele responds by withholding hats and placing one short synth-bass fifth only after the final low resonance clears. Midway through, the players reverse roles and Maialen must answer inside a narrow sequence of gaps created by Iker's faster pattern. Leire uses dry minor-seventh Pianet dyads after each complete debt repayment. The electronic beat becomes densest only when the ledger returns to zero.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old precipitation ledger open to a page labeled WEST ROOM, two graphite tally marks in column B, a wet wooden stick resting across blue ruled paper, one low-frequency waveform drawn beneath the totals. Sparse lyric fragments limited to “two calls owed,” “west room,” “pay the answer,” and “ledger zero.”

Track 3: Rain Stops Before Either Player Wins
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 112 BPM and begins with almost no drums: one rounded kick every bar, txalaparta, and faint room tone. Maialen and Iker alternate EQUAL answers so neither side establishes dominance. Grain Carry becomes increasingly important as the lower planks ring longer in the damp room, forcing Nele to mute more upper clicks and Pianet attacks. Jon uses only fingertip pandero rolls during the first half. Klara adds breath-only contrabass-flute attacks under the longest acoustic tails. In the final minute, the two players shorten their responses until the exchange feels nearly continuous, but the machine beat becomes progressively sparser through repeated Courtesy Cuts. The rain outside audibly weakens. The final phrase ends with one unanswered low-plank strike from Iker; instead of recording it as a debt, Maialen lowers her sticks. The track ends on timber decay and the last drops from the roof gutter.
